WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.167 --> 00:00:02.201
-It's nice to have you
back on the air.

00:00:02.201 --> 00:00:04.571
You returned from
a little bit of a break.

00:00:04.571 --> 00:00:06.206
I know you don't really
ever take a break.

00:00:06.206 --> 00:00:07.606
-No, we work through the breaks.

00:00:07.606 --> 00:00:09.542
We were planning some ridiculous
stories for later this year.

00:00:09.542 --> 00:00:12.245
-Yes. And I think --

00:00:12.245 --> 00:00:15.882
no one works harder than you
or your staff.

00:00:15.882 --> 00:00:17.884
And so it's very hard
to catch you on vacation.

00:00:17.884 --> 00:00:19.352
-Yeah.
-Sometimes I will see you

00:00:19.352 --> 00:00:21.787
and I'll think, "Oh, you didn't
have a show last week."

00:00:21.787 --> 00:00:24.457
Maybe you'll be refreshed, and
you just always look exhausted.

00:00:24.457 --> 00:00:26.192
-No, yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:00:26.192 --> 00:00:27.660
That's something to do with work

00:00:27.660 --> 00:00:29.762
and I do have
resting exhausted face, anyway.

00:00:29.762 --> 00:00:31.030
[ Laughter ]

00:00:31.030 --> 00:00:32.231
People do -- even when
I come back from vacation,

00:00:32.231 --> 00:00:33.900
sometimes people will go,
"Everything all right?

00:00:33.900 --> 00:00:35.401
It's tough right now, isn't it?"

00:00:35.401 --> 00:00:37.237
Well, this is three days
on a beach.

00:00:37.237 --> 00:00:38.671
-You never --
[ Laughter ]

00:00:38.671 --> 00:00:40.306
-Okay.
-As you say,

00:00:40.306 --> 00:00:42.275
you never stop working,
but what you do lose

00:00:42.275 --> 00:00:44.710
when you're on a break is
the outlet to process the news.

00:00:44.710 --> 00:00:46.246
-Yeah.
-I know even when we have

00:00:46.246 --> 00:00:48.514
a couple weeks off, that
is the hardest part for me.

00:00:48.514 --> 00:00:52.218
Can you appreciate being
unplugged from the news at all,

00:00:52.218 --> 00:00:54.319
or do you just miss being able
to talk about it?

00:00:54.319 --> 00:00:55.588
-It's nice in general.

00:00:55.588 --> 00:00:58.324
It's nice to unplug from
something that's poisonous.

00:00:58.324 --> 00:01:00.626
But the problem is,
when there's poison around,

00:01:00.626 --> 00:01:02.261
you kind of want to know
what's happening.

00:01:02.261 --> 00:01:06.132
So, no. It feels reckless
and irresponsible to say,

00:01:06.132 --> 00:01:07.933
"I won't concern myself
with the fact

00:01:07.933 --> 00:01:09.869
that America is destroying
itself.

00:01:09.869 --> 00:01:12.204
I'll surprise myself
three months later

00:01:12.204 --> 00:01:13.973
to see what's happened."

00:01:13.973 --> 00:01:17.410
No, so, it's nice to have
an outlet again.

00:01:17.410 --> 00:01:19.478
Do you struggle?
-I -- well --

00:01:19.478 --> 00:01:22.014
What I found -- I do.
-Really? Do you?

00:01:22.014 --> 00:01:23.850
-No, I mutter to myself

00:01:23.850 --> 00:01:25.484
a lot more when we
don't have the show.

00:01:25.484 --> 00:01:27.320
-Oh, really?
-You know, because this is my --

00:01:27.320 --> 00:01:29.121
this is like a very polite way

00:01:29.121 --> 00:01:30.456
to do what
would just be muttering

00:01:30.456 --> 00:01:31.491
if I didn't have a TV show.

00:01:31.491 --> 00:01:32.826
-Yeah,
it's a very erudite mutter

00:01:32.826 --> 00:01:35.494
that you vomit into a camera.
-Yeah.

00:01:35.494 --> 00:01:37.763
-So you just walk around --
-I just walk around town --

00:01:37.763 --> 00:01:40.466
-"Why was he at an omelet bar?"
[ Laughter ]

00:01:40.466 --> 00:01:42.568
-Here's the thing.
And you know, you guys

00:01:42.568 --> 00:01:45.538
obviously do stories
that are sort of longer tales

00:01:45.538 --> 00:01:47.272
as opposed to just talking about
what happened yesterday.

00:01:47.272 --> 00:01:48.374
-Right.
-What happened yesterday,

00:01:48.374 --> 00:01:49.542
if you have a week off,

00:01:49.542 --> 00:01:51.244
you don't have to pay
that much attention,

00:01:51.244 --> 00:01:53.245
because by the following Monday,

00:01:53.245 --> 00:01:56.882
nothing that matters -- nothing
that happened matters anymore.

00:01:56.882 --> 00:01:59.618
-That's it. That is the sense
where time has kind of changed.

00:01:59.618 --> 00:02:01.087
-Yeah.
-And it used to be that you

00:02:01.087 --> 00:02:03.122
could assume that if something
happened in the week

00:02:03.122 --> 00:02:07.560
that you're still in, it would
have a place in people's memory.

00:02:07.560 --> 00:02:10.629
Now, 72 hours is a long time.
-Yeah.

00:02:10.629 --> 00:02:12.932
[ Laughter ]
-So often we find ourselves

00:02:12.932 --> 00:02:15.468
saying, "Oh, and he did this
at the start of this week,

00:02:15.468 --> 00:02:17.069
this human week."
-Yes.

00:02:17.069 --> 00:02:20.239
-This actual week
that we started on Monday.

00:02:20.239 --> 00:02:23.909
And so, yeah. There's a lot --
generally we don't talk about

00:02:23.909 --> 00:02:26.078
anything in the week
that doesn't happen

00:02:26.078 --> 00:02:28.448
kind of Thursday night, Friday.
There's no point.

00:02:28.448 --> 00:02:31.351
-You know, it's nice --
you do provide a nice service

00:02:31.351 --> 00:02:33.986
in that obviously things
are crazy here right now.

00:02:33.986 --> 00:02:37.423
You do remind the world
that other places are crazy.

00:02:37.423 --> 00:02:39.359
-Yeah, but that's a tough sell,
though. That's the problem.

00:02:39.359 --> 00:02:41.694
So it's difficult when you're
doing international stories

00:02:41.694 --> 00:02:43.396
on the show, like last year,
we wanted to do a show

00:02:43.396 --> 00:02:46.232
about the Brazilian election
and it's a tough sell to say,

00:02:46.232 --> 00:02:48.701
"I know things are rough
in America right now,

00:02:48.701 --> 00:02:50.903
but we need to talk
about Brazil."

00:02:50.903 --> 00:02:53.773
And you can kind of feel going,
"Not [bleep] now we don't."

00:02:53.773 --> 00:02:55.107
[ Laughter ]

00:02:55.107 --> 00:02:57.210
It's like saying to someone
whose house is on fire,

00:02:57.210 --> 00:03:00.312
"There is a house on fire
three streets away."

00:03:00.312 --> 00:03:03.482
Any other time, I would give
a [bleep] about that.

00:03:03.482 --> 00:03:06.118
But my possessions are burning.

00:03:06.118 --> 00:03:10.023
So it's tough to kind of say,
"we need to talk about Brazil,"

00:03:10.023 --> 00:03:11.824
and not have that sense
of "Do we?"

00:03:11.824 --> 00:03:14.193
-Yeah.
-Do we definitely?

00:03:14.193 --> 00:03:15.895
-You talked about Brexit
last night.

00:03:15.895 --> 00:03:17.796
Obviously, you have
a connection to that.

00:03:17.796 --> 00:03:20.900
-Yes, yeah.
-Do you feel like that's a story

00:03:20.900 --> 00:03:25.270
that people here have any sense
of exactly how insane it is?

00:03:25.270 --> 00:03:28.641
-Well, you can -- Broadly,
you can get a sense

00:03:28.641 --> 00:03:31.477
of how insane it is, just from
being secure in the knowledge

00:03:31.477 --> 00:03:33.178
that it's [bleep] crazy
what's happening.

00:03:33.178 --> 00:03:36.382
Now, as you go into actually
what is happening,

00:03:36.382 --> 00:03:39.452
the details of it are so absurd

00:03:39.452 --> 00:03:42.955
that it kind of exacerbates
the insanity,

00:03:42.955 --> 00:03:44.791
but it does take
a long time to understand.

00:03:44.791 --> 00:03:47.360
To understand exactly
why Northern Ireland

00:03:47.360 --> 00:03:49.562
and the Northern Ireland border
is so critical here to this deal

00:03:49.562 --> 00:03:51.297
that isn't actually a deal,
it's a deal just to get to

00:03:51.297 --> 00:03:53.165
an interim period
to get to the deal --

00:03:53.165 --> 00:03:57.870
It's fun to understand all of
that if you are into masochism.

00:03:57.870 --> 00:03:59.505
[ Laughter ]

00:03:59.505 --> 00:04:03.309
But you can just reassure
that what Britain is doing

00:04:03.309 --> 00:04:05.111
is driving itself off a cliff.

00:04:05.111 --> 00:04:08.681
They are "Thelma &amp; Louise"-ing
themselves off a cliff.

00:04:08.681 --> 00:04:11.417
-The next cliff opportunity
we have here

00:04:11.417 --> 00:04:13.019
seems to be the 2020 election.
-Yes.

00:04:13.019 --> 00:04:14.887
-That seems to be
a good cliff opportunity.

00:04:14.887 --> 00:04:16.255
-Yeah.

00:04:16.255 --> 00:04:18.324
That's definitely a good time
to look over the cliff.

00:04:18.324 --> 00:04:20.626
"I don't know, we somehow
lived through the last one.

00:04:20.626 --> 00:04:22.361
Maybe we can fly."

00:04:22.361 --> 00:04:24.129
[ Laughter ]

00:04:24.129 --> 00:04:26.366
-Yeah.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:04:26.366 --> 00:04:28.834
"We're going to live forever!"
-Exactly.

00:04:28.834 --> 00:04:31.571
-You -- Obviously,
one of the things --

00:04:31.571 --> 00:04:33.272
I mean, and a lot of people
are announcing

00:04:33.272 --> 00:04:35.741
and cable news is certainly
doing a lot of coverage

00:04:35.741 --> 00:04:37.410
of 2020 already.
How do you feel about that?

00:04:37.410 --> 00:04:39.245
A little early?
-It's a little early, right?

00:04:39.245 --> 00:04:41.247
Because the bar
is pretty low now.

00:04:41.247 --> 00:04:43.716
Whenever anyone announces
for president, I just go,

00:04:43.716 --> 00:04:46.518
"Sure, fine. Yeah.
Add it to the list."

00:04:46.518 --> 00:04:48.453
You're fine.
Kamala Harris, great.

00:04:48.453 --> 00:04:49.688
Bernie sanders, great.

00:04:49.688 --> 00:04:51.791
A tortoise walking across

00:04:51.791 --> 00:04:53.826
an abandoned
Wendy's parking lot,

00:04:53.826 --> 00:04:55.428
has he got an exploratory
committee?

00:04:55.428 --> 00:04:57.497
You would work, as well.
[ Laughter ]

00:04:57.497 --> 00:04:59.499
The bar is so low.
So all they're going to do now

00:04:59.499 --> 00:05:00.699
is just announce
they're running for President

00:05:00.699 --> 00:05:01.800
and then go raise money.

00:05:01.800 --> 00:05:03.602
So there is not much
to see there.

00:05:03.602 --> 00:05:05.671
-Yeah.
-So, yeah, it's dangerous

00:05:05.671 --> 00:05:07.306
I think giving it too much
attention right now

00:05:07.306 --> 00:05:08.941
when there is plenty
of other [bleep]

00:05:08.941 --> 00:05:10.043
that we should be looking at.

00:05:10.043 --> 00:05:12.044
-What about this idea
of you want to --

00:05:12.044 --> 00:05:14.414
you know, you can get caught up
in the everyday

00:05:14.414 --> 00:05:17.216
of what's happening
with this presidency.

00:05:17.216 --> 00:05:18.618
You don't want to
get complacent.

00:05:18.618 --> 00:05:20.652
You don't want to get
desensitized to the idea

00:05:20.652 --> 00:05:22.388
that madness is happening.

00:05:22.388 --> 00:05:23.689
But you also don't want
to be screaming

00:05:23.689 --> 00:05:24.723
and yelling all the time.

00:05:24.723 --> 00:05:26.592
Like, how do you find
a balance there?

00:05:26.592 --> 00:05:29.261
-I mean, I don't know. That's --

00:05:29.261 --> 00:05:31.997
I mean, that's a kind of
existential question, isn't it?

00:05:31.997 --> 00:05:34.700
How do we all seek happiness
right now

00:05:34.700 --> 00:05:37.002
that isn't, at its root,
fraudulent?

00:05:37.002 --> 00:05:39.338
[ Light laughter,
audience "ohhs" ]

00:05:39.338 --> 00:05:42.741
Oh, God, that was
a truly depressing "ohh"

00:05:42.741 --> 00:05:44.543
from the audience.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:44.543 --> 00:05:45.611
"Listen, we're just trying to

00:05:45.611 --> 00:05:48.314
have a night out in New York,
dude.

00:05:48.314 --> 00:05:51.684
I didn't come here
for nihilism."

00:05:51.684 --> 00:05:54.687
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:05:54.687 --> 00:05:56.221
-They were like,
"John Oliver is on.

00:05:56.221 --> 00:05:57.990
He's gonna give us
an update on Brazil."

00:05:57.990 --> 00:05:59.258
[ Laughter ]

00:05:59.258 --> 00:06:02.628
-"Now he's saying we're all dust
in the wind of history."

00:06:02.628 --> 00:06:04.697
Yeah, it's difficult.
It's difficult.

00:06:04.697 --> 00:06:07.232
I think that the problem
is that at the moment, like,

00:06:07.232 --> 00:06:09.401
not only with Trump,
who is president,

00:06:09.401 --> 00:06:11.303
so you have to pay attention
to him

00:06:11.303 --> 00:06:14.874
like you would pay attention
to a bull in a china shop.

00:06:14.874 --> 00:06:18.944
But also, the new candidates,
it's a --

00:06:18.944 --> 00:06:21.280
it kind of shows that we're
concentrating too much

00:06:21.280 --> 00:06:23.115
on personalities right now,

00:06:23.115 --> 00:06:26.519
which is hard to criticize

00:06:26.519 --> 00:06:28.220
when those personalities
are in the White House.

00:06:28.220 --> 00:06:32.324
But we do have systemic problems
underneath them.

00:06:32.324 --> 00:06:35.994
So I can't really get excited
about new candidates yet

00:06:35.994 --> 00:06:37.597
because it's 20 months away
from the election.

00:06:37.597 --> 00:06:39.265
-Yeah, it's a long time away.
-It's a long time.

